title: "Ghost Infrastructure: Historical Industrial Geography and the Persistence of Path-Dependent Accessibility in Bochum, Germany"
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: "Maske"
    given-names: "Sakshi D."
repository-code: "https://github.com/sakshimaske303-commits/GHOST_INFRASTRUCTURE"
url: "https://github.com/sakshimaske303-commits/GHOST_INFRASTRUCTURE"
identifiers:
  - type: doi
    value: "10.5281/zenodo.21761320"
    description: "Zenodo archive of GHOST INFRASTRUCTURE v1.0.0"
doi: "10.5281/zenodo.21761320"
abstract: >-
  A historical-cartographic and spatial-network study testing whether Bochum's
  19th and 20th-century coal-mining industrial geography — mine locations and
  worker-housing colonies (Zechensiedlungen) — continues to structurally
  predict present-day "15-minute city" walking accessibility, more than five
  decades after the region's last coal mine closed. Thirteen historical coal
  mines and four worker colonies were digitized from archival sources, and
  present-day accessibility was modeled using true network-distance
  isochrones across Bochum's complete 69,393-node pedestrian street network.
  Contrary to the hypothesis that historical industrial sites would predict
  present-day neglect, a Welch's t-test found the opposite (t=42.887,
  p<0.00001, Cohen's d=0.589), verified against city-center proximity as a
  confound via logistic regression, and independently corroborated by a
  Local Moran's I spatial-clustering analysis showing 97.1% of
  low-accessibility nodes fall within statistically significant cold-spot
  clusters. The evidence supports a "path dependency of centrality" rather
  than a "path dependency of neglect."
keywords:
  - path dependency
  - 15-minute city
  - urban accessibility
  - historical GIS
  - network analysis
  - post-industrial geography
  - spatial statistics
  - Local Moran's I
  - Germany
  - Ruhr Valley
